{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 520,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every good nerd knows that E=mc^2. \nEvery great nerd knows that, really,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7280,
    "dDurationMs": 9560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "E^2=m2c4+p2c2. But what does that even mean? Oh, \nI’ll tell you, but today I’d like to invite you to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16840,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an even higher level of nerdom. There may be even \nmore add ons to Einstein’s famous equation that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will make even the greatest nerds quiver in their \n… space time merch if they turn out to be real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30600,
    "dDurationMs": 11440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because they could point the way to \nthe quantum structure of Space Time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A top contender for the most profound insight \nin the history of physics was a little idea by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48040,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Galileo 400 years ago. He realized that \nspeed is relative—in a fundamental way.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55040,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Galilean relativity or Galilean invariance \nstates that the laws of physics should be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60120,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same “invariant” for all inertial observers. \nThe laws of physics don’t care how fast you’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66320,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving. As long as you aren’t accelerating—that’s \nwhat inertial means. Drop a ball in a train moving  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71640,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at a constant speed and the ball falls \nstraight down from your perspective,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just as it would if you were standing on the \ntrain station—again, from your perspective. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81360,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means that you can define any reference frame as \nhaving zero velocity and describe all motion with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "respect to that frame. There’s no preferred \nnotion of stillness in Galilean relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92840,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now galilean relativity was the basis of \nIsaac Newton’s mechanics a half century later,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98080,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which changed all of physics. But Galilean \nrelativity had its glitches—for example, it proved  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105240,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inconsistent with electromagnetism as described \nby Maxwell’s equations in the mid-1800’s. Einstein  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "realized that he could fix this inconsistency by \njust being much more serious about the proposition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that laws of physics don’t care about velocity. \nHe realized that even our measurement of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124600,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed of light constitutes a law of physics. By \nrequiring that all non-accelerating observers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measure the same speed of light led him \nto his theory of special relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135120,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Galilean relativity you can change \nto a different reference frame just by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139680,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "adding the velocity of that reference \nframe to all other velocities. This is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143560,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called the Galilean transformation. But that \ncan easily lead to some things having speeds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150120,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "greater than light. This is fixed in Einsteinian \nrelativity by including this extra factor during  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157080,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that transformation so that nothing ever exceeds \nlight speed. This is the Lorentz transformation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163440,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the factor that we added is the \nLorentz factor. And the fact that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167640,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "laws of physics don’t change under a Lorentz \ntransformation is called Lorentz symmetry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173640,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A similar idea about the invariance of the laws \nof physics in accelerating frames of reference  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "versus gravitational fields gave Einstein the \ngeneral theory of relativity in 1915. But GR  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183800,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revealed a new contradiction. Galilean relativity \nwas succeeded by Einsteinian relativity, in part  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191520,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inspired by Galileo’s own insight, and motivated \nin part to fix the contradiction with Maxwell’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetism. The latter was in turn succeeded \nby quantum mechanics a decade after general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity. But now a new conflict arose—general \nrelativity turned out to be in conflict with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211280,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the new quantum mechanics, just as Galilean \nrelativity conflicted with electromagnetism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216200,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Efforts to fix this contradiction \nspawned all of our work towards a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221360,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory of quantum gravity—string \ntheory, loop quantum gravity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and many more—and we’ve covered a lot \nof these before. But maybe things don’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229240,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have to be so complicated—at least, maybe \nwe can get some insight into the answer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234480,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by doing the same thing that Einstein and \nGalileo did—by taking relativity seriously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241520,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In relativity there are two fundamental \nconstants—the speed of light “c” and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246200,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational constant “G”. With \nquantum mechanics, a new constant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250640,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of nature emerged—the Planck constant, h, or \nh-bar. When we combine these constants we get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "various so-called Planck units. These units tell \nus where quantum mechanics and general relativity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "merge—they are the scale at which spacetime \nis expected to become quantum. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268840,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum mechanics tells us that probing small \ndistances requires large energies, while GR  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274000,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tells us that the energy curves the fabric of \nspacetime, and at a certain point the curvature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278760,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is so great that black holes form. The distance \nwhere this occurs is called the Planck length,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287640,
    "dDurationMs": 9080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1.6x10^-35 m, and the energy needed to probe \nthat size is the Planck energy—2 billion Joules."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296720,
    "dDurationMs": 10320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sounds tricky, but it’s not the first time \ntwo excellent theories conflicted. I hope  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you still remember that Galilean relativity and \nNewtonian mechanics conflicted with Maxwell’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312080,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetism. And also that Einstein \nfixed this by doubling down on Galilean  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317800,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity—by taking seriously the idea \nthat the laws of physics shouldn’t depend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321440,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on velocity. So what if we take it even \nmore seriously. There’s another thing that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably shouldn’t change depending on how fast \nyou’re traveling—and that’s the Planck length."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Special relativity tells us that lengths appear \nshorter along the direction of motion—this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is length contraction, and it should apply to \nthe Planck length. But this length is the scale  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343560,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at which the fabric of spacetime itself takes on \nstrong quantum properties, which sounds pretty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349120,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamental. So maybe the Planck length shouldn’t \ndepend on the velocity of the observer. That’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "especially true because it would change only \nin the direction of motion—implying that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360920,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transition between classical and quantum spacetime \nwould depend on direction, which seems weird."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368200,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In quantum gravity theories that have some \nsort of discrete structure underlying the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372480,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smooth fabric of space, this could mean \nthat structure gets sort of flattened for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377400,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all but one reference frame. In general, a \ncontracting Planck length suggests there’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a single preferred frame of reference—the one \nwhere it’s not contracted in one direction.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390360,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One more reason to wonder if the Planck length \nshould be invariant— is that it’s defined only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396880,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by fundamental constants G, C, and h-bar. \nSo what does it even mean for it to shrink?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403160,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Lorentz transformation is a correction to \nthe Galilean transformation that makes the speed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408880,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of light invariant for all observers. \nSo what if there’s another correction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that could make the Planck length invariant \nfor all observers, regardless of velocity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it turns out there is a way to do \nthis. It was proposed by Italian physicist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424080,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Giovanni Amelino-Camelia back in 2000, \nand it’s called doubly special relativity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431800,
    "dDurationMs": 8120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or DSR. The modern version of DSR is formulated \nto actually treat the Planck energy as invariant,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439920,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than the Planck length. The Planck \nenergy is the energy you need to actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444280,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probe the Planck length, and is related to \nit with a simple formula. If we can keep the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449280,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planck energy independent of velocity then \nwe’ve done the same with the Planck length."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453880,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now because we’re now talking in \nenergies, we get to use Einstein’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459680,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equation relating energy to mass and \nmotion. You’ve definitely seen part of it:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "E=mc^2—probably the most famous equation \nin the world. The full version is this,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472160,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It says that a particle’s energy is equal \nto the energy bound up in its mass plus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the energy of its motion, expressed here in \nterms of the particle’s momentum. It’s also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484840,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called the relativistic dispersion \nrelation—which I’m telling you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488840,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because I have to keep calling it the \n“dispersion relation” for the next bit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492840,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now remember the Lorentz factor was added \nto the Galilean transformation in a way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497160,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that only makes it significant close to the \nspeed of light, we can also add this factor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504040,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the dispersion relation that only becomes \nrelevant at energies close to the Planck energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511160,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the modified dispersion relation—the \nMDR. That extra bit stands for one or more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "terms that include the ratio of particle \nenergy to the Planck energy. For energies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much lower than the Planck energy—which means \nfor most of the universe—this part becomes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "close to zero and so we just have the original \ndispersion relation. Just like how the Lorentz  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transformation becomes the Galilean transformation \nfor velocities much smaller than that of light.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538280,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The higher the power n, the closer you need to \nget to the Planck energy to observe the effect,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545120,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the coefficient eta also governs the \noverall strength of the term. If those terms  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the MDR become significant compared \nto the original terms of the dispersion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556240,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relation it would mean that energy and momentum \nconservation as we know them no longer hold."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564320,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "See these conservation laws are a \ndirect result of the Lorentz symmetry,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "via Noether’s theorem—something we covered \na while back. If Lorentz symmetry is broken  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574240,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then energy and momentum conservation also \nbreak. Which would be fine —we’d end up with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a more fundamental symmetry and more fundamental \nconserved quantities to go with it. But energy and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586360,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "momentum conservation have been pretty fundamental \nto the development of all of physics. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591360,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we use these laws to determine how particle \ninteractions happen in quantum physics. They don’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596840,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just determine how particles exchange energy and \nmomentum—they determine what interactions are even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602320,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible. And that gives us a way to potentially \ntest the ideas of doubly special relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609680,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s an example. A neat trick that the \nmassless photon can do is to turn itself  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613600,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a pair of massive particles—one of \nmatter and one of antimatter. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a photon could become an electron and positron. \nHowever, due to the normal rules of energy and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "momentum conservation, this “pair production” is \nnot possible for a single photon without help.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Pair production only happens when a photon \ninteracts with something else—often another  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photon. But if energy and momentum conservation is \nbroken due to our modification of the dispersion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relation then it becomes possible for a \nsingle photon to undergo pair production."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643200,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That would be a huge deal. \nCurrently, photons and other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massless bosons like gluons are thought \nto be completely stable in isolation. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they aren’t really stable then that’s \nsomething we could potentially test."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would take a very energetic photon—remember, \nthe modified dispersion relation starts to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663760,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "act differently approaching the Planck \nenergy. But this non-Lorentzian behavior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668440,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can still happen the further from the \nPlanck energy, albeit with less likelihood."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If some form of the modified dispersion relation \nis right, then there should be a faint possibility  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680320,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that a photon of an extremely high but not \nimpossibly high energy will decay via pair  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686520,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "production given a long enough time. And if \nany one such photon is given enough time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693760,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then that small probability becomes a large \none. That means there should be a limit to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how far a high energy photon should be able \nto travel through space before it decays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706120,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have seen very high energy photons from \nobjects pretty far away—especially from supernova  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712760,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remnants in the Milky Way Galaxy. Apparently \nthough there was not a high enough chance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719400,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of decay of these photons by pair production \nto eliminate them on their way to us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727080,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now another reaction forbidden by \nnormal conservation laws is for an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron to spontaneously emit a photon without \nfirst interacting with something else. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736360,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“vacuum cerenkov radiation” shouldn’t exist, but \nwith a modified dispersion relation it just might.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744720,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we have not seen any evidence of vacuum \ncherenkov radiation in astrophysical sources.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751400,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Between the absence of pair-production decay and \nvacuum Cherenkov radiation, researchers have been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to constrain the strength of any possible\nmodified part of the dispersion relation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763280,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So apparently we need a more senstive test."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766520,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another hotly debated prediction from some \nDSR theories is that the speed of light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771960,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might actually be different depending on its \nwavelength, and hence energy. But why should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778480,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fixing the Planck length or Planck energy \ncause the speed of light to vary? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783640,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one way to express the speed of light—or \nanything really—is as the ratio of kinetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789600,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy to momentum—and that’s c for light. But \nin our modified dispersion relation, energy has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794680,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these extra bits to it. At very high energies \nthose extra bits become important and cause the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801200,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy-momentum ratio to deviate from c. So if \nthis ratio actually defines the speed of light,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808560,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then we have a way to change that speed \nat extreme energies. Whether high energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons move faster or slower than low energy \nones depends on the nature of the modified  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819640,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dispersion relation being considered,  but \neither way this can potentially be tested."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now scientists have been looking for \ntiny energy-dependent variations in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830680,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed of light for a long time. We’ve even talked \nabout it before. One way to do this is to look  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for different arrival times of photons from the \nsame distant astrophysical event. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gamma-ray bursts are a class of uber-bright \nsupernova that produce high-energy gamma-ray  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847520,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons as well as photons at all other \nlower energies. We know that all these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851880,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons all started their journey to us at \nthe same time—the time of the explosion.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857200,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in principle we should be able to tell if \nthe higher energy ones traveled at a different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speeds—they’d arrive at a different \ntime. Perhaps earlier, perhaps later."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most recent and highest energy \never detection of such an event,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873880,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is gamma-ray burst 221009A, comes \nfrom the Large High Altitude Air  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881080,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Shower Observatory (LHAASO) in 2022. No \ndifference in arrival time of high versus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "low-energy photons was detected within the \nrather high sensitivity of this experiment.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894040,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That allowed us to place pretty tight bounds on \nany energy variability of the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901120,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that in turn places limits on the strength \nof the extra terms in our modified dispersion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907400,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relation. The researchers claim to have \n95% confidence that if there were linear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913440,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corrections to the speed of light, they’d \nhave to be at energies quite a bit larger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917720,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the Planck energy. This effectively \nrules out the strongest corrections—those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923040,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where the power—the n in our MDR—equals \none. Quadratic corrections—where n=2—-have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929600,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be at least on the energy scale \nof 10^11 GeV. That’s around 5 orders  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935800,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of magnitude more energetic than the \nmost energetic gamma ray ever detected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941600,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not so surprising that current experiments \nhaven’t detected any of the predictions of doubly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948200,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "special relativity or found evidence of the \nmodified dispersion relation. As far as we know,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953600,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "E still equals mc^2. But in this case we haven’t \nyet proved Einstein right. The potential breakdown  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961200,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Lorentz symmetry at the Planck scale is still \nan issue that we don’t understand fully, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it may be that the alterations needed to the \ndispersion relation are just too small for current  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972600,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "methods or current space explosions. But at least \nwe know how to look for these effects, which has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "been a real challenge for anything in the realm \nof quantum gravity. And it sure would be cute if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986120,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first clue to the quantum nature of space \ncame from the 400 year old insight by Galileo.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992640,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No matter how fast you go, physics looks the \nsame—even the length-scale of Planckian spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000080,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey, Everyone! You know how black holes pull \neverything in with irresistible gravity? And you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know how the upcoming holiday season often feels \nthe exact same way? Well that’s why we’d like to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009720,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "welcome you to our first ever Black Hole Friday \nSale! On Friday, November 29th, for one day only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017880,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entire Merch store catalog will be on sale for \n20% off. We also have a bunch of brand new merch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024760,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such as the first ever Multifunctional Space Time \nPen, which features a level, rulers in inches,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "centimeters, and millimeters, a stylus and micro \nscrewdriver. It’s basically a low-grade sonic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036360,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "screwdriver. Stand by for the full-tech version. \nIf you need somewhere to use that pen, We also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040040,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a new Standard Model LED night light \nfor when you’re working on your theories  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1044400,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "late into the evening, a new Neural Net desktop \nmat to keep your computer stylish while your run  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050800,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your simulations, a 520 Piece Puzzle for when \nyou’re waiting on those simulations to render,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058000,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our first ever Poster that will remind you of \nwhere all elements came from, and a Penrose  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064280,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Diagram Black Hole Rocks Glass, for when you need \na tasty beverage to celebrate of your successful  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory. Can we legally say buying Space Time \nmerch will help you formulate a Nobel Prize  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075600,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "winning physics theory? That’s a grey area. But \nwe can offer you 20% off every t-shirt, hoodie,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patch, pin we’ve sold this year during their \nspecial re-appearance on the Merch store. which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087960,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you could theoretically wear to holiday events \nor to accept any kind of important international  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1094400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prize. And speaking of the non-Americans, if \nyou're one of our many international viewers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1100720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "November 29th will be that last date we can \nguarantee international delivery before December  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106560,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "25th. So if you need a gift for any special person \nin the galaxy, including yourself, remember to go  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1113800,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to PBSspacetime.com/shop on November 29th \nfor our 1st ever Black Hole Friday sale!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
